```
function splitUnion(t: string): string[] {
  // split on ' | ' not inside <> or ()
  let depth=0; const parts=[]; let cur=''
  for (let i=0;i<t.length;i++){
    const c=t[i]
    if (c==='<'||c==='(') depth++
    else if (c==='>'||c===')') depth--
    if (depth===0 && c==='|' && t[i-1]===' ' && t[i+1]===' ') { parts.push(cur.trim()); cur=''; i++; continue }
    cur+=c
  }
  if (cur.trim()) parts.push(cur.trim())
  return parts
}
```
Hmm iterating and skipping spaces. Let me just do a simpler regex-free approach. Actually simpler: split on '|' then trim, but `Record<string, unknown>` has no '|'. Nested unions inside `(A | B)[]` do have '|' inside parens. For mergeTypes I mostly deal with leaf types like 'number','string','null','boolean','Date','unknown','{}','Record<string, unknown>','unknown[]', or object interface names. These have no '|'. For nested `(A | B)[]` merging rarely needed. I'll implement depth-aware.

